CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure shows a 3D diagram illustrating the Riemann sum approximation used to define the double integral as a volume. A curved surface z = f(x, y) is shown in xyz-space above a region in the xy-plane; the base is gridded to represent a partition into subrectangles. A single representative rectangular parallelepiped is highlighted in orange: its base is a subrectangle with area Delta_Ak and sampling point (x*_k, y*_k) labeled, and its height is f(x*_k, y*_k), giving volume f(x*_k, y*_k) Delta_Ak. Annotations label Height f(x*_k, y*_k), Volume f(x*_k, y*_k) Delta_Ak, and Area Delta_Ak. The figure introduces the double integral as the limit of a Riemann sum of such parallelepiped volumes over all subrectangles as the partition is refined.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Black x-axis arrow pointing toward lower-left (front), labeled 'x'
  - Black y-axis arrow pointing right, labeled 'y'
  - Black z-axis arrow pointing up, labeled 'z'
  - Thin light-gray square outline representing the visible patch of the xy-plane, drawn below/right of the origin
  - Bean/kidney-shaped closed region R in the xy-plane with a thick teal outline and pale teal fill
  - Rectangular grid of thin gray lines covering R (the partition into subrectangles), clipped to the region
  - Translucent teal curved surface z = f(x, y) floating above the region, with a wavy free edge on its near side
  - Label 'z = f(x, y)' in the upper right of the frame
  - Translucent pale-gray vertical 'cylinder' wall swept from the boundary curve of R up to the surface
  - One highlighted orange rectangular parallelepiped (column) standing on a single subrectangle of R, rising to the surface
  - Darker orange top face of the column where it meets the surface
  - Red dot at the sample point (xk*, yk*) on the base subrectangle
  - Red dot at the point (xk*, yk*, f(xk*,yk*)) on the top face
  - Red dashed vertical segment joining the two red dots (the column height)
  - Leader arrow + text 'Height f(xk*, yk*)' pointing at the dashed segment from the right
  - Leader arrow + text 'Volume f(xk*, yk*)ΔAk' pointing at the column body from the lower right
  - Leader arrow + text 'Area ΔAk' pointing at the base subrectangle from below left
  - Leader arrow + text '(xk*, yk*)' pointing at the lower red dot from below right

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World axes: +X points front-left (drawn toward lower-left, labeled x), +Y points right (labeled y), +Z up (labeled z); origin at the axis corner on the left of the frame. Axis arrow lengths: x ≈ 3.2, y ≈ 4.2, z ≈ 3.6 units, black (#1a1a1a), ~0.035 shaft radius with cone arrowheads. Visible xy-plane patch: thin light-gray (#c8c8c8) square outline spanning x ∈ [-0.6, 3.0], y ∈ [-0.4, 3.2], lying at z = 0 to the right/front of the origin. Region R: closed kidney/bean curve centered at (xc, yc) = (1.2, 1.2) with polar radius r(θ) = 1.05 + 0.18·cos(2θ) - 0.30·cos(θ) (max extent ≈ 1.4), thick teal outline #0fa37f (~0.04 tube), fill pale teal #d6ecea at ~55% opacity. Partition grid: axis-aligned lines spacing (2.8/n) over the bounding box of R, drawn only inside R, gray #9aa5a5 at 0.5 opacity; each cell is ΔAk = (2.8/n)^2 (default n=6 → ΔAk ≈ 0.218). Surface z = f(x,y): sampled on x ∈ [-0.4, 2.8], y ∈ [-0.4, 3.2], clipped so its footprint is slightly larger than R, translucent teal #8ec9c3 at ~0.55 opacity, double-sided, with a faint darker teal rim; it sits roughly 1.8–2.8 units above the plane for the default f. Vertical 'cylinder': the boundary curve of R extruded straight up to the surface, near-white/pale gray #e9eef0 at ~0.22 opacity, no top cap. Orange column: base = one grid cell (side 2.8/n) containing the sample point, extruded from z = 0 up to z = f(xk*, yk*); body orange #f2a349 at ~0.5 opacity, edges #d9822b, top face solid orange #e8952f at 0.8 opacity. Default sample point (xk*, yk*) = (1.2, 1.15). Red dots (#e33127, radius ≈ 0.07) at (xk*, yk*, 0) and (xk*, yk*, f(xk*,yk*)), joined by a red dashed segment (dash 0.12, gap 0.09). Labels in dark gray/black sans-serif with thin black leader arrows: 'z = f(x, y)' top-right of frame above the surface; 'Height f(xk*, yk*)' at right, arrow pointing left to the midpoint of the dashed segment; 'Volume f(xk*, yk*)ΔAk' at lower right, arrow pointing up-left to the mid-height of the column; 'Area ΔAk' at bottom-left, arrow pointing up-right to the base cell; '(xk*, yk*)' at bottom-right of the base, arrow pointing up-left to the lower red dot. Live numeric readout of f(xk*,yk*), ΔAk and their product should accompany the labels.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "That the orange solid is a genuine 3D box standing between a flat base subrectangle and the curved surface, not a 2D rectangle drawn on the picture"
  },
  {
    "id": "surfaceEquation",
    "type": "equation_input",
    "teaches": "That the integrand f is what sets the height of every approximating column: changing the formula reshapes the surface and instantly rescales the column height f(xk*,yk*) and its volume f(xk*,yk*)·ΔAk",
    "label": "Surface z = f(x, y)",
